# Commercial S10 – inherited S8 backup hash drift

## Symptom

The full Control regression suite failed in
`test_all_installed_material_is_hash_bound`: the canonical
`tu1nz_adult_public_s8_backup.sh` SHA-256 was
`d2d38842bfbeab29d4d1a56bfd34b90fbda271925e259ad38fb09788225f8bc5`,
while the S8 manifest still expected
`7fdc50abdc25214f606712723538d1126d587f89de46f0df3b089c29f8d2d795`.

## Cause

Canonical Control commit `d63fd6b532a9b5a190d308505b04b843f8fab3e5`
extended the shared S8 backup script so the later S9 configuration, migration,
units and timers are included in the recovery surface. The historical S8 file
binding was not refreshed in that commit. The script bytes in the working tree
and `origin/control-main` are identical, proving this is inherited manifest
drift rather than an uncommitted local modification.

## Resolution and risk

Only `files.backup_script_sha256` in the S8 manifest is updated to the SHA-256
of the already canonical shared backup script. No script, unit, runtime,
credential, server or product boundary changes. This restores fail-closed
hash verification without changing the S8 or S9 operational surface.

Rollback is the normal Git revert of this manifest-only correction. A server
rollback is neither required nor authorized.
